        'How a transformer works showed two kinds of loss. No-load losses run 8,760 hours a year whatever the load. Load losses grow with the load squared. A secondary substation transformer often averages 30 to 50% of its rating, so over a year its no-load losses often match or exceed its load losses.\n\nEfficiency peaks where the two are equal: at a load of √(P0 / Pk), with P0 the no-load and Pk the full-load losses. For a Tier 2 630 kVA unit that is √(0.54 / 4.6) ≈ 34%.',
        'Because Europe has millions of these units, the EU caps their losses. The Ecodesign Regulation for transformers, (EU) 548/2014, amended by 2019/1783, set limits in two steps: Tier 1 from July 2015 and Tier 2 from July 2021.\n\nFor a 630 kVA distribution transformer up to 24 kV, Tier 2 allows at most 540 W no-load and 4,600 W load losses; Tier 1 allowed 600 W and 6,500 W. The amendment added exceptions, for example where a Tier 2 unit would not fit an existing station at reasonable cost.',
        'Buyers go beyond the legal limit by putting a price on each kilowatt of loss: loss capitalisation. Offers are compared on total cost = price + A × P0 + B × Pk, with A and B in euros per kW. A is the value today of a kilowatt lost all year for the whole life. B is smaller, because load losses only run in full at full load: roughly A times the average load squared.\n\nPresent value and discount rate are explained in Cost engineering.',
        'With A at about €10,000 to €14,000 per kW (energy at €0.06 to €0.08 per kWh over 40 years at 4%), saving 300 W of no-load loss is worth €3,000 to €4,000: often more than the extra price of a better core.\n\nAn amorphous core, made of a thin glassy metal ribbon instead of grain-oriented steel, can cut no-load losses by more than half. It costs more, is heavier and somewhat noisier. Experts disagree on how far below Tier 2 it pays to go.',
